/// \defgroup grp_w1012_gui_wnd_script_parsers Window layout (.wnd) field parsers
/// \ingroup grp_gui_apt
/// \brief The functions that turn the fields of a .wnd window layout entry
///        into window settings, gadget data and a screen rectangle.
///
/// A .wnd file is a text layout script for the legacy window system
/// (GameWindowManager): a tree of WINDOW entries, each describing one window
/// or gadget, with CHILD blocks for nested windows. The loader in
/// GameWindowManagerScript.cpp reads one entry at a time. That loader is
/// described here only by its contract; the functions documented on this
/// page are the leaves it calls.
///
/// How a window entry is read. This is Zero Hour's parseWindow; BFME 2's
/// retail window parser calls getDataTemplate(), parseScreenRect() and the
/// DATA parser in the same order, walks the same kind of keyword table and,
/// for a keyword not in the table, checks the same three special keywords
/// (DATA, END and CHILD).
/// 1. The window's settings start from defaults in a stack-local
///    WinInstanceData (WinInstanceData::init).
/// 2. The WINDOWTYPE line names the window or gadget type (PUSHBUTTON,
///    SCROLLLISTBOX, USER and so on), and getDataTemplate() returns the
///    scratch structure for that type's gadget-specific data, or null.
/// 3. The SCREENRECT line goes to parseScreenRect(), which yields the
///    window's position and size.
/// 4. Every following field is a keyword, an equals sign and a value that
///    runs to the next semicolon. The loader looks the keyword up in a fixed
///    table of keyword and parser pairs and calls the parser with the
///    keyword, the instance data, the value text and the gadget data
///    pointer. A parser that returns false abandons the entry.
/// 5. A keyword not in the table is checked against DATA (read the legacy
///    gadget data, see \ref grp_w1012_gui_wnd_script_parsers_legacy_data
///    "The legacy DATA field", then carry on with the next field), END
///    (apply the header-template font, create the window if CHILD has not
///    already created it, and finish the entry) and CHILD (create the
///    window, then parse its children); anything else is skipped up to its
///    semicolon. In BFME 2's retail window parser, too, only the END path
///    asks the header template manager for a font, and END creates the
///    window only when none exists yet.
///
/// The keyword table in BFME 2's retail data holds, in order: NAME, STATUS,
/// STYLE, SYSTEMCALLBACK, WINCLASS, INPUTCALLBACK, TOOLTIPCALLBACK,
/// DRAWCALLBACK, FONT, HEADERTEMPLATE, LISTBOXDATA, COMBOBOXDATA,
/// SLIDERDATA, RADIOBUTTONDATA, TOOLTIPTEXT, TOOLTIPDELAY, TEXT, TEXTCOLOR,
/// STATICTEXTDATA, TEXTENTRYDATA, TABCONTROLDATA, twenty-four draw-data
/// keywords (all handled by parseDrawData()), IMAGEOFFSET and TOOLTIP. It
/// is Zero Hour's table with WINCLASS added. The callback, WINCLASS,
/// TEXTENTRYDATA and TOOLTIP parsers are not covered here.
///
/// The parsers fall into three kinds:
/// - Instance-data parsers write the window's own WinInstanceData: name and
///   id (parseName()), status and style bits (parseStatus(), parseStyle()),
///   font (parseFont()), header template (parseHeaderTemplate()), text and
///   tooltip labels (parseText(), parseTooltipText()), tooltip delay
///   (parseTooltipDelay()), text colours (parseTextColor()), draw-state
///   images and colours (parseDrawData()) and image offset
///   (parseImageOffset()).
/// - Gadget-data parsers fill the scratch structure from getDataTemplate():
///   parseListboxData(), parseComboBoxData(), parseSliderData(),
///   parseRadioButtonData(), parseStaticTextData() and
///   parseTabControlData(). They cast the data pointer to the type they
///   expect and do not check that the window type matches, so a gadget-data
///   field on the wrong window type writes the wrong structure (or, with no
///   template, dereferences null).
/// - Helpers: scanBool() and its integer siblings, parseBitString() and
///   parseBitFlag() for flag lists, parseColor() for default colours and
///   setWindowText() for putting a window's translated label on screen
///   after it is created.
///
/// Every field parser on this page, plus parseScreenRect(),
/// parseBitString() and parseColor(), tokenises with the C library's strtok
/// (parseStatus() and parseStyle() through parseBitString()), so within one
/// thread they share a single tokenizer position (strtok's saved state).
/// scanBool(), parseBitFlag(), setWindowText() and getDataTemplate() do not
/// call strtok. A parser starts tokenising its own value buffer, except
/// parseScreenRect(),
/// which continues the tokenizer the loader started on the SCREENRECT
/// line. Most fields are read by
/// position: a parser reads a label token and throws it away, then reads
/// the value, so the labels written in a layout (IMAGE:, COLOR:, SIZE: and
/// so on) are never compared. The one exception covered here is the
/// optional ScrollIfAtEnd label in parseListboxData().
///
/// The bodies are Zero Hour's GameWindowManagerScript.cpp parsers, carried
/// through the BFME 1 reconstruction; each block below notes where BFME 2's
/// body differs. In Zero Hour all of these live in
/// GameWindowManagerScript.cpp; the reconstruction may hold them in
/// separate units, so use the file links on each function rather than
/// assuming one file.
///
/// \anchor grp_w1012_gui_wnd_script_parsers_legacy_data
/// \par The legacy DATA field
/// DATA gives a gadget's creation data as a bare list of numbers, outside
/// the keyword table and the per-gadget fields (LISTBOXDATA, SLIDERDATA and
/// so on). The window-entry loader hands a DATA value to its own parser,
/// parseData, and BFME 2's retail loader still contains that call. Zero
/// Hour's source marks parseData obsolete and says it is never reached;
/// whether any BFME 2 layout still writes a DATA field has not been
/// checked. The value is split on blanks and read with atoi, by position:
/// - VERTSLIDER, HORZSLIDER: minimum and maximum value.
/// - SCROLLLISTBOX: list length, auto-scroll, auto-purge, scroll bar,
///   multi-select and force-select.
/// - ENTRYFIELD: maximum text length, an ignored width, a secret-text flag
///   (normalised to true or false; false when absent) and an optional
///   character filter, where 1 means numbers only, 2 letters and numbers
///   and 3 ASCII only.
/// - STATICTEXT: a centred flag (normalised); the rest is ignored.
/// - RADIOBUTTON: the radio group number.
///
/// \par
/// The results go into parseData's own static structures, not the
/// getDataTemplate() ones, and the window's gadget data pointer is
/// redirected to the filled structure. The slider, list box and entry
/// structures are cleared first; the static text and radio button ones are
/// not. For any other type, including COMBOBOX and TABCONTROL, the data
/// pointer is set to null, which discards that window's template.
/// parseData always returns true. BFME 2 keeps the entry-field character
/// filter as bits in a flags word, the same bits parseComboBoxData() and
/// the TEXTENTRYDATA parser set, where Zero Hour sets three separate Bool
/// members.
///
/// \par Modding
/// - Window layouts, not code, decide which windows exist, their
///   positions, fonts, colours, images, text labels, tooltips and which
///   named callbacks drive them. Text labels are string-table keys:
///   parseTooltipText() and setWindowText() look them up through the game
///   text interface (TheGameText), so wording and translation are string
///   table edits. Images are looked up by name in the mapped image
///   collection (parseDrawData()).
/// - Hard-coded: the keyword list above and the status and style flag
///   names (parseStatus(), parseStyle()); nine draw-data slots per state;
///   text and tooltip labels must be shorter than 128 characters, and a
///   longer one makes the parser fail, which abandons the window entry
///   (Zero Hour's loader); flag lists and font names are
///   copied into 256-character stack buffers without a length check; the
///   PANEDISABLED count in TABCONTROLDATA is not checked against the
///   number of tab panes.
/// - Values are parsed with sscanf or atoi and are not validated. A
///   missing value token is passed on as a null pointer, which these
///   helpers do not check.
/// - These parsers build client-side GUI state and touch no GameLogic
///   objects. The shared engine services they can add entries to include
///   the name-key table (parseName(); Zero Hour: nameToKey adds names it
///   has not seen) and, in Zero Hour, the font library (parseFont(); getFont
///   loads and keeps a font it has not loaded yet) and the string table's
///   list of missing labels (parseTooltipText(); fetch records a label it
///   cannot find). Whether BFME 2's multiplayer file checks cover .wnd
///   files has not been verified.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// - Layouts are authored at a creation resolution, and parseScreenRect()
///   scales x and y independently to the current display size, so it
///   stretches a 4:3 layout to fill a wider screen rather than letterboxing
///   it. parseFont() passes FONT sizes to the font library as written and
///   applies no resolution scaling itself.
/// - The parsers keep state in globals: the function-local template
///   structures of getDataTemplate() and of the DATA parser, and the shared
///   sub-gadget draw-data arrays filled by parseDrawData(). Layout loading
///   is therefore not safe to run on more than one thread at a time. The
///   strtok position is per-thread state in the multithreaded C runtime
///   the game imports it from, but it is still one position per thread, so
///   nested or interleaved tokenising on one thread is unsafe.
/// - Colours are packed 32-bit values with alpha in the top byte, then red,
///   green and blue; each channel keeps only its low eight bits.
///
/// \see \ref sub_gui_apt, \ref grp_w2_gui_window_text, \ref grp_w1_gui_gadgets


/// \file GUI/GameWindowManagerScriptDrawData.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w1012_gui_wnd_script_parsers
