/// \fn void *getDataTemplate(char *type, Int *sizeOut)
/// \brief Returns the scratch gadget-data structure for a window type, and
///        optionally its size.
///
/// Each gadget type that needs type-specific creation data has one
/// function-local static structure here: sliders (VERTSLIDER and
/// HORZSLIDER share one), SCROLLLISTBOX, TABCONTROL, ENTRYFIELD,
/// STATICTEXT, RADIOBUTTON and COMBOBOX. The window-entry loader asks for
/// the template right after reading WINDOWTYPE (BFME 2's retail parser
/// calls it there too). Zero Hour's loader then passes it to every field
/// parser as the data pointer, so the gadget-data parsers fill it, and
/// hands it to window creation. BFME 2's retail code also calls this function
/// from a window-manager routine that rebuilds a gadget from an existing
/// window: it uses the returned size to copy the source window's gadget data
/// over the template before creating the new gadget.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// The type is compared case-sensitively with strcmp. For a known type the
/// size of its structure is written through sizeOut when sizeOut is not
/// null, the first four bytes of the structure are cleared and its address
/// is returned. For any other type the result is null and sizeOut is left
/// alone.
///
/// \param type     Window type name from the WINDOWTYPE line, such as
///                 "SCROLLLISTBOX".
/// \param sizeOut  [out] Receives the size in bytes of the returned
///                 structure; may be null.
/// \return The type's static template, or null for a type without gadget
///         data (USER, PUSHBUTTON, CHECKBOX, PROGRESSBAR and others).
///
/// \note BFME 2 differs from Zero Hour in two ways. Zero Hour takes only
/// the type and clears the whole structure; BFME 2 adds the size output and
/// clears only the first four bytes. Fields that a window's data lines do
/// not set therefore keep whatever the previous window of the same type
/// left in the shared static.
/// \warning The returned structure is shared by every window of that type
/// and is overwritten by the next window entry. Whoever creates the window
/// must copy what it needs.
/// \see \ref grp_w1012_gui_wnd_script_parsers_legacy_data
///      "The legacy DATA field", parseListboxData, parseTabControlData


/// \fn Bool parseScreenRect(char *token, char *buffer, Int *x, Int *y, Int *width, Int *height)
/// \brief Reads a window's SCREENRECT, scales it to the current display and
///        returns its position relative to the parent and its size.
///
/// SCREENRECT is the second required line of every window entry. It gives
/// the upper-left and bottom-right corners in screen pixels, plus the
/// resolution the layout was authored at:
/// UPPERLEFT: x y, BOTTOMRIGHT: x y, CREATIONRESOLUTION: width height.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - Tokenising continues from the loader's own strtok call on the
///   SCREENRECT line (the separators include blanks, commas, colons and the
///   equals sign); token and buffer are not read. The three label tokens
///   are skipped and the six numbers read with the integer scan helper.
/// - Both corners are multiplied by display width over creation width and
///   display height over creation height (from TheDisplay), and truncated
///   to integers.
/// - If a parent window is open on the loader's window stack, the position
///   is made relative to the parent's screen position; otherwise it is the
///   screen position. Width and height are the scaled corner differences.
///
/// \param token   The SCREENRECT keyword; unused.
/// \param buffer  The line buffer; unused (see above).
/// \param x       [out] Left edge, relative to the parent if there is one.
/// \param y       [out] Top edge, relative to the parent if there is one.
/// \param width   [out] Scaled width in pixels.
/// \param height  [out] Scaled height in pixels.
/// \return Always true.
///
/// \note The body is Zero Hour's. In BFME 2's retail code the two unused
/// parameters do not exist at all: the compiler gave this file-local
/// function a private calling convention that passes only the four
/// outputs.
/// \warning A creation resolution of zero is not guarded against.
/// \par Remastering
/// Of the parsers on this page, this is the only one that adapts a layout
/// to the display: x and y scale independently, so aspect ratio is not
/// preserved, and the results are whole pixels.
/// \see getDataTemplate


/// \fn Bool parseName(char *token, WinInstanceData *instData, char *buffer, void *data)
/// \brief Handles NAME: stores the window's decorated name and derives its
///        window id from it.
///
/// The quoted name becomes the instance data's decorated name string, and
/// NameKeyGenerator::nameToKey turns it into the window id
/// (TheNameKeyGenerator). Code finds a window by computing the same key
/// from the same string, so the name is how callbacks and screens address a
/// layout's windows. Zero Hour's code looks windows up by names of the form
/// "File.wnd:WindowName".
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Skips to the first double quote and takes the text up to the next one.
/// The id is only assigned when the name key generator exists.
///
/// \param token     The NAME keyword; unused.
/// \param instData  [out] Window settings being built.
/// \param buffer    The value text; must contain a double quote.
/// \param data      Gadget data; unused.
/// \return Always true.
/// \par Modding
/// Renaming a window in a layout breaks every piece of code that looks it
/// up by the old name.
/// \warning A value with no double quote makes the scan run off the end of
/// the buffer. The same applies to parseFont(), parseHeaderTemplate(),
/// parseText() and parseTooltipText().
/// \see parseHeaderTemplate, parseText


/// \fn Bool parseStatus(char *token, WinInstanceData *instData, char *buffer, void *data)
/// \brief Handles STATUS: replaces the window's status bits with the flags
///        named in the value.
///
/// Clears the status word, then sets one bit per flag name in a list such
/// as ENABLED+IMAGE (see parseBitString()). The name table is the status
/// flag list, whose position gives the bit. In BFME 2's retail data it
/// holds Zero Hour's twenty-six names (ACTIVE, TOGGLE, DRAGABLE, ENABLED,
/// HIDDEN, ... ON_MOUSE_DOWN) followed by three more, spelled with their
/// enum prefix: WIN_STATUS_CIRCULAR, WIN_STATUS_AVAIL_FLAG and
/// WIN_STATUS_BLOCK_INPUT.
///
/// \param token     The STATUS keyword; unused.
/// \param instData  [out] Window settings being built.
/// \param buffer    Flag names joined by plus signs, or the word NULL.
/// \param data      Gadget data; unused.
/// \return Always true; unknown names are ignored.
/// \par Modding
/// The bit for each name is its position in a compiled-in table, so a new
/// status flag needs code, and the names must be spelled exactly as in the
/// table (matching ignores case).
/// \see parseStyle, parseBitString


/// \fn Bool parseStyle(char *token, WinInstanceData *instData, char *buffer, void *data)
/// \brief Handles STYLE: replaces the window's style bits with the flags
///        named in the value.
///
/// Works like parseStatus() with the style name table, which in BFME 2's
/// retail data is unchanged from Zero Hour: PUSHBUTTON, RADIOBUTTON,
/// CHECKBOX, VERTSLIDER, HORZSLIDER, SCROLLLISTBOX, ENTRYFIELD, STATICTEXT,
/// PROGRESSBAR, USER, MOUSETRACK, ANIMATED, TABSTOP, TABCONTROL, TABPANE
/// and COMBOBOX. The style bits are what gadget code and setWindowText()
/// test to decide what kind of gadget a window is.
///
/// \param token     The STYLE keyword; unused.
/// \param instData  [out] Window settings being built.
/// \param buffer    Flag names joined by plus signs, or the word NULL.
/// \param data      Gadget data; unused.
/// \return Always true; unknown names are ignored.
/// \see parseStatus, parseBitString


/// \fn void parseBitString(const char *inBuffer, UnsignedInt *bits, const char **flagList)
/// \brief Sets one bit for each name in a plus-separated flag list.
///
/// Copies the input to a local buffer so the caller's text is not
/// changed. A list starting with NULL means no flags. Otherwise each name
/// between plus signs is looked up with parseBitFlag(). Bits are only ever
/// set, never cleared; the STATUS and STYLE parsers clear the word first.
///
/// \param inBuffer  Flag list such as "ENABLED+IMAGE"; not modified.
/// \param bits      [in,out] Word that receives the bits.
/// \param flagList  Null-terminated table of names; the index of a name is
///                  its bit number.
///
/// \note The body tokenises its copy with strtok, so it resets the shared
/// tokenizer position. Unknown names are skipped without a message.
/// \warning The local copy is 256 characters and is made without a length
/// check.
/// \see parseBitFlag, parseStatus, parseStyle


/// \fn Bool parseBitFlag(const char *flagString, UnsignedInt *bits, const char **flagList)
/// \brief Looks one flag name up in a name table and sets the matching bit.
///
/// Walks the null-terminated table, comparing case-insensitively. On the
/// first match it ORs in the bit whose number is the name's index.
///
/// \param flagString  One flag name.
/// \param bits        [in,out] Word that receives the bit.
/// \param flagList    Null-terminated name table.
/// \return True if the name was found, false otherwise.
///
/// \note In BFME 2's retail code this function exists as its own body but
/// is also expanded inline into parseBitString(), its only user.
/// \see parseBitString


/// \fn Bool parseFont(char *token, WinInstanceData *instData, char *buffer, void *data)
/// \brief Handles FONT: asks the font library for the named font and makes
///        it the window's font.
///
/// The value has the shape NAME: "Font Name", SIZE: n, BOLD: n. The quoted
/// name, the size and the bold flag are passed to FontLibrary::getFont
/// (TheFontLibrary); the window's font is changed only when a font comes
/// back. When the entry reaches END without a CHILD block, a header
/// template that yields a font replaces this font before the window is
/// created (see parseHeaderTemplate()). A window whose entry has a CHILD
/// block is created when CHILD is read, before that step, so it keeps its
/// FONT value. This is Zero Hour's loader; BFME 2's retail window parser
/// follows the same END and CHILD order.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// The name is the text after the first double quote, up to the next quote
/// or separator, and is copied into a 256-character local buffer without a
/// length check. Size and bold are read with the integer scan helper.
///
/// \param token     The FONT keyword; unused.
/// \param instData  [out] Window settings being built.
/// \param buffer    The value text.
/// \param data      Gadget data; unused.
/// \return Always true, including when the font is not found.
///
/// \note BFME 2's font library takes the size as a floating-point value,
/// where Zero Hour's takes an integer.
/// \par Remastering
/// The size is used as written, with no resolution scaling here.
/// \see parseHeaderTemplate


/// \fn Bool parseHeaderTemplate(char *token, WinInstanceData *instData, char *buffer, void *data)
/// \brief Handles HEADERTEMPLATE: stores the name of the window's header
///        template.
///
/// Stores the quoted name in the instance data. Header templates are named
/// font definitions kept by the header template manager
/// (TheHeaderTemplateManager). When the entry reaches END without a CHILD
/// block, a template that yields a font replaces the FONT value before the
/// window is created. A window whose entry has a CHILD block is created when
/// CHILD is read, before that step, so it keeps its FONT value and the
/// template's font is not applied to it. This is Zero Hour's loader; BFME
/// 2's retail window parser follows the same END and CHILD order.
///
/// \param token     The HEADERTEMPLATE keyword; unused.
/// \param instData  [out] Window settings being built.
/// \param buffer    The value text; must contain a double quote.
/// \param data      Gadget data; unused.
/// \return Always true.
/// \see parseFont, parseName


/// \fn Bool parseText(char *token, WinInstanceData *instData, char *buffer, void *data)
/// \brief Handles TEXT: stores the window's text label, the string-table
///        key for the text it shows.
///
/// Only the label is stored here. The text itself is set once the window
/// exists, by setWindowText(), which looks the label up in the string
/// table.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Takes the text between the first double quote and the next quote, tab
/// or line break. A label of 128 characters or more is rejected.
///
/// \param token     The TEXT keyword; unused.
/// \param instData  [out] Window settings being built.
/// \param buffer    The value text; must contain a double quote.
/// \param data      Gadget data; unused.
/// \return True, or false for a label that is too long, which makes the
///         loader abandon the window entry.
///
/// \note An empty value (two adjacent quotes) leaves no token, and the
/// length check is then made on a null pointer. parseTooltipText() guards
/// this case; this function does not.
/// \see setWindowText, parseTooltipText


/// \fn Bool parseTooltipText(char *token, WinInstanceData *instData, char *buffer, void *data)
/// \brief Handles TOOLTIPTEXT: stores the tooltip label and sets the
///        tooltip to its string-table text.
///
/// Unlike parseText(), the lookup happens at parse time: the label is
/// stored, then GameTextInterface::fetch (TheGameText) translates it and
/// WinInstanceData::setTooltipText stores the result in the instance data's
/// tooltip display string.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Takes the text between the first double quote and the next quote, tab
/// or line break. An empty value (only the closing quote left) returns
/// true without a tooltip. A label of 128 characters or more is rejected.
///
/// \param token     The TOOLTIPTEXT keyword; unused.
/// \param instData  [out] Window settings being built.
/// \param buffer    The value text; must contain a double quote.
/// \param data      Gadget data; unused.
/// \return True, or false for a label that is too long, which makes the
///         loader abandon the window entry.
/// \see parseTooltipDelay, parseText


/// \fn Bool parseTooltipDelay(char *token, WinInstanceData *instData, char *buffer, void *data)
/// \brief Handles TOOLTIPDELAY: stores the window's tooltip delay.
///
/// The value is a single integer with no label, stored as is. Without the
/// field the delay keeps the -1 that WinInstanceData::init sets, which
/// Zero Hour's comment calls the default (Zero Hour's loader calls init
/// before reading fields). The unit of other values is not established
/// here.
///
/// \param token     The TOOLTIPDELAY keyword; unused.
/// \param instData  [out] Window settings being built.
/// \param buffer    The value text.
/// \param data      Gadget data; unused.
/// \return Always true.
/// \see parseTooltipText


/// \fn Bool parseTextColor(char *token, WinInstanceData *instData, char *buffer, void *data)
/// \brief Handles TEXTCOLOR: reads the text and text-border colours for the
///        enabled, disabled and highlighted states.
///
/// The value holds three pairs in a fixed order (enabled, disabled,
/// highlighted), each a colour and a border colour of four numbers, red,
/// green, blue and alpha, each pair element preceded by a label token that
/// is skipped. The colours go into the instance data's three text draw
/// records.
///
/// \param token     The TEXTCOLOR keyword; unused.
/// \param instData  [out] Window settings being built.
/// \param buffer    The value text.
/// \param data      Gadget data; unused.
/// \return Always true.
///
/// \note BFME 2 packs the channels inline, keeping the low eight bits of
/// each in alpha, red, green, blue order, where Zero Hour calls
/// GameMakeColor. The packed result is the same.
/// \see parseDrawData, parseColor


/// \fn Bool parseDrawData(char *token, WinInstanceData *instData, char *buffer, void *data)
/// \brief Handles the twenty-four draw-data keywords: an image, a colour
///        and a border colour for each of the nine slots of one draw state.
///
/// The keyword decides the destination:
/// - ENABLEDDRAWDATA, DISABLEDDRAWDATA and HILITEDRAWDATA fill the window's
///   own enabled, disabled and highlighted draw-data arrays.
/// - The LISTBOX..., SLIDERTHUMB... and COMBOBOX... keywords fill
///   engine-wide global arrays (external linkage) shared by all windows:
///   the list box up button, down
///   button and slider, the slider thumb, and the combo box drop-down
///   button, edit box and list box, each with enabled, disabled and
///   highlighted variants. Zero Hour: gadget creation copies these arrays
///   into the child windows it builds for list boxes, sliders and combo
///   boxes; the arrays are shared, not kept per window.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// For each of the nine slots the parser reads IMAGE: name, COLOR: r g b a
/// and BORDERCOLOR: r g b a by position. An image named NoImage clears the
/// slot's image; any other name is looked up with
/// ImageCollection::findImageByName (TheMappedImageCollection); Zero
/// Hour's lookup returns null for an unknown name, which also leaves the
/// slot without an image. Colours are packed with GameMakeColor.
///
/// \param token     The keyword, which selects the destination array.
/// \param instData  [out] Window settings being built.
/// \param buffer    The value text.
/// \param data      Gadget data; unused.
/// \return True, or false for a keyword it does not know (only reachable
///         if the keyword table and this function disagree).
///
/// \note The body is Zero Hour's, unchanged.
/// \par Modding
/// All nine slots must be present in the value; what each slot means is
/// up to the gadget's draw function, not this parser. Image names must
/// match entries in the mapped image collection.
/// \see parseTextColor, parseImageOffset


/// \fn Bool parseImageOffset(char *token, WinInstanceData *instData, char *buffer, void *data)
/// \brief Handles IMAGEOFFSET: stores the x and y offset at which the
///        window's image is drawn.
///
/// Two integers, read with atoi, become the instance data's image offset.
///
/// \param token     The IMAGEOFFSET keyword; unused.
/// \param instData  [out] Window settings being built.
/// \param buffer    The value text.
/// \param data      Gadget data; unused.
/// \return Always true.
/// \see parseDrawData


/// \fn Bool parseListboxData(char *token, WinInstanceData *instData, char *buffer, void *data)
/// \brief Handles LISTBOXDATA: fills the list box creation data, including
///        the column layout.
///
/// Values, in order (Zero Hour's label names): LENGTH, the list length;
/// AUTOSCROLL; an optional ScrollIfAtEnd flag; AUTOPURGE; SCROLLBAR;
/// MULTISELECT; COLUMNS, the column count; one width per column when there
/// is more than one column; FORCESELECT.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - ScrollIfAtEnd is the only label any of these parsers compares (case
///   insensitively); without it the flag is false and the label just read
///   belongs to AUTOPURGE.
/// - With more than one column, an integer array of column-width
///   percentages is allocated with new and filled; otherwise that pointer
///   is null. The column pixel-width pointer is always reset to null.
///
/// \param token     The LISTBOXDATA keyword; unused.
/// \param instData  Window settings; unused.
/// \param buffer    The value text.
/// \param data      [out] The list box template from getDataTemplate().
/// \return Always true.
///
/// \note The body is Zero Hour's; only BFME 2's structure layout differs.
/// Zero Hour: the list box gadget deletes the width array when it is
/// destroyed, so the gadget created from the entry owns it.
/// \see getDataTemplate,
///      \ref grp_w1012_gui_wnd_script_parsers_legacy_data
///      "The legacy DATA field"


/// \fn Bool parseComboBoxData(char *token, WinInstanceData *instData, char *buffer, void *data)
/// \brief Handles COMBOBOXDATA: fills the combo box creation data.
///
/// Values, in order, each after a skipped label: editable flag, maximum
/// characters, maximum entries shown before scrolling, ASCII-only flag and
/// letters-and-numbers-only flag.
///
/// \param token     The COMBOBOXDATA keyword; unused.
/// \param instData  Window settings; unused.
/// \param buffer    The value text.
/// \param data      [out] The combo box template from getDataTemplate().
/// \return Always true.
///
/// \note BFME 2 changes how the two character filters are stored. Zero
/// Hour keeps them as two Bool members; BFME 2 clears a flags word and sets
/// one bit for each filter that is on. The same bits mean ASCII only and
/// letters and numbers only in the entry-field data read from a DATA
/// field (see \ref grp_w1012_gui_wnd_script_parsers_legacy_data
/// "The legacy DATA field").
/// \see getDataTemplate


/// \fn Bool parseSliderData(char *token, WinInstanceData *instData, char *buffer, void *data)
/// \brief Handles SLIDERDATA: reads a slider's minimum and maximum values.
///
/// Two values after skipped labels (Zero Hour: MINVALUE and MAXVALUE). The
/// body is Zero Hour's.
///
/// \param token     The SLIDERDATA keyword; unused.
/// \param instData  Window settings; unused.
/// \param buffer    The value text.
/// \param data      [out] The slider template from getDataTemplate().
/// \return Always true.
/// \see getDataTemplate


/// \fn Bool parseRadioButtonData(char *token, WinInstanceData *instData, char *buffer, void *data)
/// \brief Handles RADIOBUTTONDATA: reads a radio button's group number.
///
/// One value after a skipped label (Zero Hour: GROUP). Zero Hour: radio
/// buttons that share a group number are one set of choices. The body is
/// Zero Hour's.
///
/// \param token     The RADIOBUTTONDATA keyword; unused.
/// \param instData  Window settings; unused.
/// \param buffer    The value text.
/// \param data      [out] The radio button template from getDataTemplate().
/// \return Always true.
/// \see getDataTemplate,
///      \ref grp_w1012_gui_wnd_script_parsers_legacy_data
///      "The legacy DATA field"


/// \fn Bool parseStaticTextData(char *token, WinInstanceData *instData, char *buffer, void *data)
/// \brief Handles STATICTEXTDATA: reads whether static text is centred.
///
/// One value after a skipped label (Zero Hour: CENTERED).
///
/// \param token     The STATICTEXTDATA keyword; unused.
/// \param instData  Window settings; unused.
/// \param buffer    The value text.
/// \param data      [out] The static text template from getDataTemplate().
/// \return Always true.
///
/// \note Zero Hour's version also sets vertical centring and left and top
/// margins of 7 here; the BFME 2 body sets only the centred flag.
/// \see getDataTemplate


/// \fn Bool parseTabControlData(char *token, WinInstanceData *instData, char *buffer, void *data)
/// \brief Handles TABCONTROLDATA: reads the tab layout and which panes
///        start disabled.
///
/// Values, in order, each after a skipped label (Zero Hour's names):
/// TABORIENTATION, TABEDGE, TABWIDTH, TABHEIGHT, TABCOUNT, PANEBORDER, then
/// PANEDISABLED with a count followed by that many flags, one per pane. The
/// tab control gadget uses these when it lays out its tabs and creates its
/// panes (see \ref grp_w1_gui_gadgets).
///
/// \param token     The TABCONTROLDATA keyword; unused.
/// \param instData  Window settings; unused.
/// \param buffer    The value text.
/// \param data      [out] The tab control template from getDataTemplate().
/// \return Always true.
///
/// \note The body is Zero Hour's.
/// \warning The PANEDISABLED count is not checked against the number of
/// pane slots, so a larger count writes past the flag array.
/// \see getDataTemplate


/// \fn Int scanBool(const char *source, Bool &val)
/// \brief Reads an integer from text and stores whether it is non-zero.
///
/// The Bool member of the scan helpers the gadget-data parsers use; its
/// siblings are scanShort, scanInt and scanUnsignedInt. It scans with
/// sscanf and the %d format into a local zero, so text that is not a number
/// gives false.
///
/// \param source  Text to scan; must not be null.
/// \param val     [out] True for any non-zero number, otherwise false.
/// \return sscanf's result: 1 when a number was read.
///
/// \note The body is Zero Hour's. In BFME 2's retail code the linker
/// folded the identical int and unsigned int versions into one body.
/// \see parseListboxData, parseComboBoxData, parseTabControlData


/// \fn Bool parseColor(Color *color, char *buffer)
/// \brief Reads three numbers as red, green and blue and stores an opaque
///        colour.
///
/// Its caller is parseDefaultColor, which reads a layout's default colour
/// fields: the word TRANSPARENT is handled there and any other value comes
/// here. The channels are read with atoi, truncated to bytes and packed with GameWindowManager::winMakeColor (TheWindowManager)
/// with full alpha.
///
/// \param color   [out] The packed colour.
/// \param buffer  Text holding the three numbers; modified by tokenising.
/// \return Always true.
/// \see parseTextColor


/// \fn void setWindowText(GameWindow *window, AsciiString textLabel)
/// \brief Looks a window's text label up in the string table and gives the
///        text to the window in the way its gadget type expects.
///
/// Window creation calls this with the label parseText() stored, once the
/// window exists.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// An empty label does nothing. Otherwise the label is translated with
/// GameTextInterface::fetch (TheGameText) and the style bits choose the
/// setter, tested in this order: push button, radio button, check box,
/// static text, entry field, and otherwise GameWindow::winSetText. An entry
/// field is the exception: it gets the label itself, widened to Unicode,
/// not the translated text.
///
/// \param window     The newly created window; must not be null.
/// \param textLabel  String-table key from the TEXT field.
///
/// \note The body is Zero Hour's. In BFME 2's retail code it is a
/// file-local function with a private calling convention, reached only from
/// window creation.
/// \see parseText, parseTooltipText
